# Byte-exact evidence must not depend on who checks it out.
#
# This repo had no .gitattributes at all, and `core.autocrlf = true` on the Windows
# maintainer machine therefore rewrote every tracked `reports/*.json` to CRLF on
# checkout.  The seal contract in tests/taiji_native/test_b0_rule_revision_seal_contract.py
# compares `sha256(path.read_bytes())` against digests that were all recorded from that
# CRLF working tree -- all 8 of them.  On CI (Linux, LF checkout) the same commit produced
# different bytes and every one of those assertions failed:
#
#   assert _digest(path) == expected
#   AssertionError: taiji_p5_2b_group_causal_corpora_20260913.json was rewritten after the
#   seal was taken
#   assert 'bcc72d366889...5c9e1cd81c6eb' == '531cf6efedb0...b44a065f5939e'
#
# The green side of this was an accident of the maintainer's platform, not a property of
# the evidence.  `text eol=lf` makes the checkout match the blob everywhere, so a sealed
# digest means the same thing on every platform; the stored expectations were then
# re-taken from the blob rather than from a platform-specific working copy.
#
# Scoped to reports/*.json because that is where the byte-exact seals live.  The blobs
# are already LF, so this introduces no content change -- only reproducibility.
reports/*.json text eol=lf
# PLAN76 v5 is sealed as exact LF bytes; training shards are binary gzip.
plans/manifests/n1_fresh_confirmation_v5_20261016.json text eol=lf
reports/taiji_n1_fresh_paired_train_20261016_s*.json.gz binary

# PLAN77 running writer emits a final CRLF: preserve its first sealed bytes verbatim.
reports/taiji_n1_fresh_paired_train_20261016.json -text !eol
